WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:04.710
Kwezi Qika is one of the best longboard surfers
in the world.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:09.290
In fact, he could almost be number one - if
you take his story into account.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:13.940
Kwezi learned to surf in the turbulent Atlantic
Ocean - even before he could swim.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.800
„I really wanted to try the sport for a
long time but obviously I couldn’t swim,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.760
so I had to prolong my time of surfing.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:32.020
It was very, very scary when I eventually
overcame the fear of not knowing how to swim

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.680
and stepping up and going in the water and
being in the water

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.380
where I can't swim. But I was so dedicated that I looked past that."

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.720
Longboard surfing is often seen as more "artistic
and graceful" than the shortboard version.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.660
As a young boy Kwezi used to sit on the beach
and watch the surfers.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.940
Back then surfing was seen as more of a "white
person's" domain.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.110
But that didn't stop Kwezi.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.860
Nor did his lack of experience in the water:

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.500
“I sat on the beach watching them and I

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.920
was so fascinated by these nice turns they
were doing and they would come out and talk

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.900
about how nice turns they had and I felt so
out.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.840
So then I told them listen I actually want
to try and he said really and gave me my first

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.000
board and suit and showed me the basics on
the beach and I was hooked.”

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.020
That was when Kwezi was 9 years old.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.980
Ever since then he and his surfboard have
been inseparable.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.619
He actually surfed for three years before
he even learned to swim - all he had to depend

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.680
on to save him out in the water was his board.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.320
But it wasn’t just any old board…

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:48.460
“I was lucky, my mom actually bought me
a board which cost her, I think, close to

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:53.160
800 Rand but she managed to get that for me
and that board was my life.”

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.720
That was a lot of money in the poverty-stricken
Ocean View Township back then.

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:01.079
But it was money well-invested.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.960
Gang crime is rife there and many of Kwezi's
old friends spent time in jail.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.720
“It was a good thing because it kept him
busy all the time.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.160
Away from the streets and from the bad things.”

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.620
Kwezi has become a popular trainer for affluent

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.760
would-be surfers along Cape Town’s beaches.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.170
He also has sponsoring contracts.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:31.180
But his heart is with Ocean View, and the
kids there who attend his free lessons:

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:37.360
“Go to school, surf, cause basically when
you surf you get yourself tired you know.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.280
Just go out there and it’s just you and
the ocean, you understand, nothing, nothing

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.280
is stopping you there.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:47.200
That’s when you are free you know, it’s
like your office and I think that was my call.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.800
Actually even if you’re staying in the hood
doesn’t mean your dreams are crushed you

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.580
know, there’s still potential, anything
is possible, you just got to have a vision”

00:02:54.580 --> 00:03:00.300
“To be like Kwezi, to stay out of trouble
and do stuff of your own and go on with your life.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.800
Don’t do wrong stuff…”

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.360
That is Kwezi’s most important message to
young people.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.860
With donated boards and wetsuits, Kwezi takes
the kids down the same path that he once took

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.569
on his way to becoming one of Africa’s first
world-class surfers.

